{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_42","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport zipfile, json, re\nz=zipfile.ZipFile('/workspace/tmp/capsule.zip')\nnb=json.loads(z.read('CapsuleNotebook-9e52daf6-ca58-43e8-e732-fbac3459d295/CapsuleNotebook-9e52daf6-ca58-43e8-e732-fbac3459d295_executed.ipynb'))\nfor i,c in enumerate(nb['cells']):\n src=''.join(c.get('source',[]))\n outs=c.get('outputs',[])\n print('\\\\nCELL',i,c.get('cell_type'))\n print('SOURCE:\\\\n',src[:5000])\n for o in outs:\n  if 'text' in o: print('TEXT:', ''.join(o['text'])[:3000])\n  if 'data' in o:\n   for k,v in o['data'].items():\n    print(k, str(v)[:2000])\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_42","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport zipfile, json, re\nz=zipfile.ZipFile('/workspace/tmp/capsule.zip')\nnb=json.loads(z.read('CapsuleNotebook-9e52daf6-ca58-43e8-e732-fbac3459d295/CapsuleNotebook-9e52daf6-ca58-43e8-e732-fbac3459d295_executed.ipynb'))\nfor i,c in enumerate(nb['cells']):\n src=''.join(c.get('source',[]))\n outs=c.get('outputs',[])\n print('\\\\nCELL',i,c.get('cell_type'))\n print('SOURCE:\\\\n',src[:5000])\n for o in outs:\n  if 'text' in o: print('TEXT:', ''.join(o['text'])[:3000])\n  if 'data' in o:\n   for k,v in o['data'].items():\n    print(k, str(v)[:2000])\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"\nCELL 0 markdown\nSOURCE:\n # Getting started\nThis is a working notebook for generating data analysis capsules for the FutureHouse Evals team. You'll find basic instructions for how to use this notebook below.\n\nIf you are not familiar with Colab Notebooks, please visit the welcome notebook at: https://colab.research.google.com/notebooks/intro.ipynb. In short, they function similarly to Jupyter Notebooks, but are more shareable for our purpose.\n\nYou may write code in either Python or R. The default is Python, but you may use R on a cell-by-cell basis using the code example below, or in the whole notebook by changing the Runtime in the tab at the top of the notebook.\n\n## Basic instructions\nThis notebook has been created for your specific use, so you should work directly in it.\n\nWe ask that you are as verbose as possible in your descriptions of various steps, as well as in your in-line code comments. This includes reasoning about the steps taken and decisions made for using various tools. Feel free to make liberal use of both in-line comments and text cells for this.\n\nIt is also helpful if you are liberal in your use of code cells, e.g. using one cell per function, rather than including too much code together in a single cell.\n\nAs always, if you have any questions do not hesitate to reach out to Jon via Slack or email (jon@futurehouse.org)\n\nCELL 1 markdown\nSOURCE:\n \n\n---\n\nCELL 2 markdown\nSOURCE:\n ## If using R, read below. Otherwise skip to [Analysis](#scrollTo=vA60UJdshKhw)\n\nCELL 3 markdown\nSOURCE:\n \nIf you will be using R in some part of your analysis, run the cell below first. You can then include `%%R` at the top of any cell to run it as R code rather than Python.\n\nIf your entire analysis will be R, you can change the notebook runtime in the Runtime tab above.\n\nCELL 4 code\nSOURCE:\n ## loading rmagic to enable R\n## to use R in a cell, put %%R at the top\n%load_ext rpy2.ipython\n\nCELL 5 code\nSOURCE:\n ## Example of R code cell\n\n%%R      ## put this at the top of a cell to run R code inside it\nx <- 42\nprint(x)\nTEXT: [1] 42\n\n\nCELL 6 markdown\nSOURCE:\n \n\n---\n\n\n\nCELL 7 markdown\nSOURCE:\n # Analysis\n\nCELL 8 markdown\nSOURCE:\n ### Package loading\n\nCELL 9 code\nSOURCE:\n ## Please install and load all external packages and libraries below this cell.\n\nCELL 10 code\nSOURCE:\n # Library packages\nlibrary(tidyverse)\nlibrary(broom)\nlibrary(dplyr)\nlibrary(splines)\nTEXT: ── \u001b[1mAttaching core tidyverse packages\u001b[22m ──────────────────────── tidyverse 2.0.0 ──\n\u001b[32m✔\u001b[39m \u001b[34mdplyr    \u001b[39m 1.1.4     \u001b[32m✔\u001b[39m \u001b[34mreadr    \u001b[39m 2.1.5\n\u001b[32m✔\u001b[39m \u001b[34mforcats  \u001b[39m 1.0.0     \u001b[32m✔\u001b[39m \u001b[34mstringr  \u001b[39m 1.5.1\n\u001b[32m✔\u001b[39m \u001b[34mggplot2  \u001b[39m 3.5.1     \u001b[32m✔\u001b[39m \u001b[34mtibble   \u001b[39m 3.2.1\n\u001b[32m✔\u001b[39m \u001b[34mlubridate\u001b[39m 1.9.3     \u001b[32m✔\u001b[39m \u001b[34mtidyr    \u001b[39m 1.3.1\n\u001b[32m✔\u001b[39m \u001b[34mpurrr    \u001b[39m 1.0.2     \n── \u001b[1mConflicts\u001b[22m ────────────────────────────────────────── tidyverse_conflicts() ──\n\u001b[31m✖\u001b[39m \u001b[34mdplyr\u001b[39m::\u001b[32mfilter()\u001b[39m masks \u001b[34mstats\u001b[39m::filter()\n\u001b[31m✖\u001b[39m \u001b[34mdplyr\u001b[39m::\u001b[32mlag()\u001b[39m    masks \u001b[34mstats\u001b[39m::lag()\n\u001b[36mℹ\u001b[39m Use the conflicted package (\u001b[3m\u001b[34m<http://conflicted.r-lib.org/>\u001b[39m\u001b[23m) to force all conflicts to become errors\n\n\nCELL 11 markdown\nSOURCE:\n ### Data loading\n\nCELL 12 code\nSOURCE:\n ## Please load any external input data or define locations for local input data below this cell.\n\nCELL 13 code\nSOURCE:\n # Import data\n# The data are a table of image metrics including colony area and circularity. These metrics were generated using imageJ and pictures of petri dishes of P. aeruginosa swarming after 48 hours.\nRaw_swarm <- read_csv(\"Swarm_2.csv\")\nTEXT: \u001b[1mRows: \u001b[22m\u001b[34m45\u001b[39m \u001b[1mColumns: \u001b[22m\u001b[34m7\u001b[39m\n\u001b[36m──\u001b[39m \u001b[1mColumn specification\u001b[22m \u001b[36m────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\u001b[39m\n\u001b[1mDelimiter:\u001b[22m \",\"\n\u001b[31mchr\u001b[39m (3): StrainNumber, Ratio, Replicate\n\u001b[32mdbl\u001b[39m (4): DishNumber, Area, Circularity, Round\n\n\u001b[36mℹ\u001b[39m Use `spec()` to retrieve the full column specification for this data.\n\u001b[36mℹ\u001b[39m Specify the column types or set `show_col_types = FALSE` to quiet this message.\n\n\nCELL 14 markdown\nSOURCE:\n ### Analysis code\n\nCELL 15 code\nSOURCE:\n ## Begin analysis code below this cell.\n\nCELL 16 code\nSOURCE:\n ## Tidy up data for downstream model fitting\n# Create dataframe for downstream model fitting\ntidy_area <- Raw_swarm %>%\n  filter(!StrainNumber %in% c(\"1\", \"98\")) %>%\n  separate(Ratio, into = c(\"rhlI_D\", \"lasI_D\"), sep = \":\", convert = TRUE) %>%\n  mutate(Frequency_rhlI = rhlI_D / (rhlI_D + lasI_D))\n\nCELL 17 code\nSOURCE:\n ### To statistically determine whether co-culturing ΔrhlI and ΔlasI can lead to improved fitness in comparison to wildtype, we have to first determine an appropriate model to describe the data since this would give us confidence intervals for our estimates.\n### For each model we quantify peak fitness as well as the confidence intervals. We also assess how well a model performs based on the R-squared and F-statistic values.\n\n## Test whether a quadratic fit is the best model to describe the data and to use to estimate peak fitness\n# Fit the quadratic regression model: area ~ frequency + frequency^2\nquadratic_model <- lm(Area ~ poly(Frequency_rhlI, 2, raw = TRUE), data = tidy_area)\n\n# Get the summary of the model to check coefficients\nsummary(quadratic_model)\n\n# Get tidy output of model coefficients\nmodel_tidy <- tidy(quadratic_model)\n\n# Estimate peak fitness (the vertex of the quadratic curve)\n# For a quadratic fit y = b0 + b1*x + b2*x^2, the peak occurs at x = -b1 / (2 * b2)\nb1 <- coef(quadratic_model)[\"poly(Frequency_rhlI, 2, raw = TRUE)1\"]\nb2 <- coef(quadratic_model)[\"poly(Frequency_rhlI, 2, raw = TRUE)2\"]\n\n# Calculate the peak fitness frequency\npeak_frequency <- -b1 / (2 * b2)\n\n# Calculate the corresponding peak fitness\npeak_fitness <- predict(quadratic_model, newdata = data.frame(Frequency_rhlI = peak_frequency))\npeak_fitness_ci <- predict(quadratic_model, newdata = data.frame(Frequency_rhlI = peak_frequency), interval = \"confidence\")\n\n# Output the peak fitness and frequency\ncat(\"Peak fitness occurs at frequency (quadratic):\", peak_frequency, \"\\n\")\ncat(\"Peak fitness (mean area) at this frequency (quadratic):\", peak_fitness, \"\\n\")\n\n# Get confidence intervals for quadratic fit\n# Confidence intervals for the coefficients\nconf_int <- confint(quadratic_model)\n\n# Print confidence intervals for model coefficients\nprint(conf_int)\ntext/plain ['\\n', 'Call:\\n', 'lm(formula = Area ~ poly(Frequency_rhlI, 2, raw = TRUE), data = tidy_area)\\n', '\\n', 'Residuals:\\n', '   Min     1Q Median     3Q    Max \\n', '-56577 -43577  -2502  24119 150261 \\n', '\\n', 'Coefficients:\\n', '                                     Estimate Std. Error t value Pr(>|t|)    \\n', '(Intercept)                           -179562      57938  -3.099 0.003754 ** \\n', 'poly(Frequency_rhlI, 2, raw = TRUE)1   833440     196481   4.242 0.000148 ***\\n', 'poly(Frequency_rhlI, 2, raw = TRUE)2  -592770     147826  -4.010 0.000293 ***\\n', '---\\n', 'Signif. codes:  0 ‘***’ 0.001 ‘**’ 0.01 ‘*’ 0.05 ‘.’ 0.1 ‘ ’ 1\\n', '\\n', 'Residual standard error: 47300 on 36 degrees of freedom\\n', 'Multiple R-squared:  0.3501,\\tAdjusted R-squared:  0.314 \\n', 'F-statistic: 9.696 on 2 and 36 DF,  p-value: 0.000428\\n']\nTEXT: Peak fitness occurs at frequency (quadratic): 0.7030045 \nPeak fitness (mean area) at this frequency (quadratic): 113393.9 \n                                         2.5 %     97.5 %\n(Intercept)                          -297065.9  -62058.09\npoly(Frequency_rhlI, 2, raw = TRUE)1  434957.8 1231921.60\npoly(Frequency_rhlI, 2, raw = TRUE)2 -892574.1 -292965.53\n\n\nCELL 18 code\nSOURCE:\n ## Test whether a cubic fit is the best model to describe the data and to use to estimate peak fitness\n# Fit the cubic regression model: area ~ frequency + frequency^2 + frequency^3\ncubic_model <- lm(Area ~ poly(Frequency_rhlI, 3, raw = TRUE), data = tidy_area)\n\n# Get the summary of the model to inspect coefficients\nsummary(cubic_model)\n\n# Get tidy output of model coefficients\nmodel_tidy_cubic <- tidy(cubic_model)\n\n# Estimate the critical points of the cubic fit\n# Extract the coefficients\nb0_cubic <- coef(cubic_model)[1]  # Intercept\nb1_cubic <- coef(cubic_model)[2]  # Linear term\nb2_cubic <- coef(cubic_model)[3]  # Quadratic term\nb3_cubic <- coef(cubic_model)[4]  # Cubic term\n\n# Solve b1 + 2 * b2 * x + 3 * b3 * x^2 = 0 for x to find critical points\ncritical_points_cubic <- polyroot(c(b1_cubic, 2 * b2_cubic, 3 * b3_cubic))\n\n# Real part of the critical points (ignoring imaginary parts)\ncritical_points_cubic <- Re(critical_points_cubic)\n\n# Classify critical points using the second derivative\n# The second derivative is 2 * b2 + 6 * b3 * x\nsecond_derivative_cubic <- 2 * b2_cubic + 6 * b3_cubic * critical_points_cubic\n\n# Identify which critical points correspond to maxima (where second derivative < 0)\nmaxima_points_cubic <- critical_points_cubic[second_derivative_cubic < 0]\n\n# Calculate the corresponding fitness values at the maximum point\npeak_fitness_cubic <- predict(cubic_model, newdata = data.frame(Frequency_rhlI = maxima_points_cubic))\n\n# Output the maximum fitness and corresponding frequency\ncat(\"Maximum fitness occurs at frequencies (cubic):\", maxima_points_cubic, \"\\n\")\ncat(\"Maximum fitness (mean area) at these frequencies (cubic):\", peak_fitness_cubic, \"\\n\")\n\n# Confidence intervals for the peak fitness\npeak_fitness_ci_cubic <- predict(cubic_model, newdata = data.frame(Frequency_rhlI = maxima_points_cubic), interval = \"confidence\")\n\n# Output the confidence intervals for the maxima points\ncat(\"Confidence intervals for peak fitness (cubic):\\n\")\nprint(peak_fitness_ci_cubic)\n\n# Get confidence intervals for the cubic fit\nconf_int_cubic <- confint(cubic_model)\n\n# Display confidence intervals for cubic model coefficients\nprint(conf_int_cubic)\ntext/plain ['\\n', 'Call:\\n', 'lm(formula = Area ~ poly(Frequency_rhlI, 3, raw = TRUE), data = tidy_area)\\n', '\\n', 'Residuals:\\n', '   Min     1Q Median     3Q    Max \\n', '-53456 -34031  -4133  23412 123015 \\n', '\\n', 'Coefficients:\\n', '                                     Estimate Std. Error t value Pr(>|t|)    \\n', '(Intercept)                            428376     143268   2.990 0.005079 ** \\n', 'poly(Frequency_rhlI, 3, raw = TRUE)1 -2731969     809836  -3.373 0.001826 ** \\n', 'poly(Frequency_rhlI, 3, raw = TRUE)2  5399762    1340054   4.030 0.000287 ***\\n', 'poly(Frequency_rhlI, 3, raw = TRUE)3 -3050542     679450  -4.490 7.41e-05 ***\\n', '---\\n', 'Signif. codes:  0 ‘***’ 0.001 ‘**’ 0.01 ‘*’ 0.05 ‘.’ 0.1 ‘ ’ 1\\n', '\\n', 'Residual standard error: 38210 on 35 degrees of freedom\\n', 'Multiple R-squared:  0.5876,\\tAdjusted R-squared:  0.5523 \\n', 'F-statistic: 16.62 on 3 and 35 DF,  p-value: 6.979e-07\\n']\nTEXT: Maximum fitness occurs at frequencies (cubic): 0.8127801 \nMaximum fitness (mean area) at these frequencies (cubic): 137097.7 \nConfidence intervals for peak fitness (cubic):\n       fit    lwr      upr\n1 137097.7 114531 159664.4\n                                          2.5 %     97.5 %\n(Intercept)                            137525.5   719226.6\npoly(Frequency_rhlI, 3, raw = TRUE)1 -4376022.5 -1087915.1\npoly(Frequency_rhlI, 3, raw = TRUE)2  2679306.8  8120217.2\npoly(Frequency_rhlI, 3, raw = TRUE)3 -4429899.2 -1671185.1\n\n\nCELL 19 code\nSOURCE:\n ## Test whether a spline fit is the best model to describe the data and to use to estimate peak fitness\n# Fit the spline regression model using natural splines\nspline_model <- lm(Area ~ ns(Frequency_rhlI, df = 4), data = tidy_area)\n\n# Get the summary of the spline model to inspect coefficients\nsummary(spline_model)\n\n# Get tidy output of model coefficients\nmodel_tidy_spline <- tidy(spline_model)\n\n# Predict peak fitness over a range of frequencies\n# Create a sequence of frequencies over the range of the data\nfrequency_seq <- seq(min(tidy_area$Frequency_rhlI), max(tidy_area$Frequency_rhlI), length.out = 1000)\n\n# Predict the area (fitness) values over this sequence\npredicted_fitness_spline <- predict(spline_model, newdata = data.frame(Frequency_rhlI = frequency_seq), interval = \"confidence\")\n\n# Find the maximum predicted fitness\nmax_index_spline <- which.max(predicted_fitness_spline[, \"fit\"])\npeak_frequency_spline <- frequency_seq[max_index_spline]\npeak_fitness_spline <- predicted_fitness_spline[max_index_spline, \"fit\"]\npeak_fitness_ci_spline <- predicted_fitness_spline[max_index_spline, c(\"lwr\", \"upr\")]\n\n# Output the peak fitness and corresponding frequency for the spline model\ncat(\"Peak fitness occurs at frequency (spline):\", peak_frequency_spline, \"\\n\")\ncat(\"Peak fitness (mean area) at this frequency (spline):\", peak_fitness_spline, \"\\n\")\ncat(\"Confidence interval for peak fitness (spline):\", peak_fitness_ci_spline, \"\\n\")\n\n# Get confidence intervals for model coefficients\nconf_int_spline <- confint(spline_model)\n\n# Print confidence intervals for spline model coefficients\nprint(conf_int_spline)\ntext/plain ['\\n', 'Call:\\n', 'lm(formula = Area ~ ns(Frequency_rhlI, df = 4), data = tidy_area)\\n', '\\n', 'Residuals:\\n', '   Min     1Q Median     3Q    Max \\n', '-55201 -16451  -2835   5311  66854 \\n', '\\n', 'Coefficients:\\n', '                            Estimate Std. Error t value Pr(>|t|)    \\n', '(Intercept)                    17014      12950   1.314    0.198    \\n', 'ns(Frequency_rhlI, df = 4)1    99058      20217   4.900 2.32e-05 ***\\n', 'ns(Frequency_rhlI, df = 4)2   227356      29490   7.710 5.77e-09 ***\\n', 'ns(Frequency_rhlI, df = 4)3    49805      35083   1.420    0.165    \\n', 'ns(Frequency_rhlI, df = 4)4   -23378      18568  -1.259    0.217    \\n', '---\\n', 'Signif. codes:  0 ‘***’ 0.001 ‘**’ 0.01 ‘*’ 0.05 ‘.’ 0.1 ‘ ’ 1\\n', '\\n', 'Residual standard error: 26580 on 34 degrees of freedom\\n', 'Multiple R-squared:  0.8062,\\tAdjusted R-squared:  0.7834 \\n', 'F-statistic: 35.35 on 4 and 34 DF,  p-value: 1.131e-11\\n']\nTEXT: Peak fitness occurs at frequency (spline): 0.9076577 \nPeak fitness (mean area) at this frequency (spline): 184371.8 \nConfidence interval for peak fitness (spline): 157912.4 210831.3 \n                                 2.5 %   97.5 %\n(Intercept)                  -9303.689  43331.8\nns(Frequency_rhlI, df = 4)1  57973.139 140143.4\nns(Frequency_rhlI, df = 4)2 167424.326 287287.6\nns(Frequency_rhlI, df = 4)3 -21491.686 121101.4\nns(Frequency_rhlI, df = 4)4 -61112.428  14356.3\n\n\nCELL 20 code\nSOURCE:\n # Plot quadratic fit\nggplot(tidy_area, aes(x = Frequency_rhlI, y = Area)) +\